   "source": [
    "---\n",
    "## 1. Basic (Primitive) Types\n",
    "\n",
    "These hold exactly one value each: int, float, str, bool, and NoneType."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 13,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "25 <class 'int'>\n",
      "3.14 <class 'float'>\n",
      "Hello <class 'str'>\n",
      "True <class 'bool'>\n",
      "None <class 'NoneType'>\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "# int -> whole numbers, no decimal point\n",
    "age = 25\n",
    "\n",
    "# float -> numbers with a decimal point\n",
    "price = 3.14\n",
    "\n",
    "# str -> text, always wrapped in quotes\n",
    "name = \"Hello\"\n",
    "\n",
    "# bool -> only two possible values: True or False\n",
    "is_active = True\n",
    "\n",
    "# NoneType -> represents 'no value yet' / empty / missing\n",
    "result = None\n",
    "\n",
    "# print() shows the value, type() shows what data type it is\n",
    "print(age, type(age))\n",
    "print(price, type(price))\n",
    "print(name, type(name))\n",
    "print(is_active, type(is_active))\n",
    "print(result, type(result))"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "---\n",
    "## 2. Collection Types\n",
    "\n",
    "These hold multiple values together: list, dictionary, tuple, and set."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# LIST -> ordered, changeable (mutable), accessed by index (position), allows duplicates\n",
    "fruits = [\"apple\", \"banana\", \"cherry\"]\n",
    "\n",
    "print(fruits[0])          # first item -> index 0 -> 'apple'\n",
    "print(fruits[-1])         # last item -> 'cherry'\n",
    "\n",
    "fruits.append(\"mango\")    # add an item to the end\n",
    "fruits[1] = \"blueberry\"   # change the item at index 1\n",
    "fruits.remove(\"apple\")    # remove a specific item by value\n",
    "\n",
    "print(fruits)              # see the list after all the changes\n",
    "print(len(fruits))         # number of items currently in the list"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# DICTIONARY -> stores key-value pairs, changeable (mutable), accessed by key (not position)\n",
    "student = {\"name\": \"Ali\", \"age\": 25, \"course\": \"Data Science\"}\n",
    "\n",
    "print(student[\"name\"])     # look up the value stored under the key 'name'\n",
    "\n",
    "student[\"age\"] = 26         # update an existing key's value\n",
    "student[\"city\"] = \"Mogadishu\"  # add a brand new key-value pair\n",
    "\n",
    "print(student.keys())       # all the keys in the dictionary\n",
    "print(student.values())     # all the values in the dictionary\n",
    "print(student.get(\"email\", \"N/A\"))  # safe lookup: returns 'N/A' if the key doesn't exist"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# TUPLE -> ordered, but immutable (cannot be changed after creation), accessed by index\n",
    "location = (37.77, -122.41)  # (latitude, longitude)\n",
    "\n",
    "print(location[0])  # 37.77 -> access like a list, by index\n",
    "\n",
    "# location[0] = 40.0   # uncommenting this line would raise an error - tuples can't be modified\n",
    "\n",
    "lat, lon = location   # 'unpacking' -> splits the tuple into two separate variables\n",
    "print(lat, lon)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# SET -> unordered, unique values only (duplicates auto-removed), changeable (mutable)\n",
    "ids = {101, 102, 102, 103}   # notice 102 is written twice but will only appear once\n",
    "print(ids)                    # {101, 102, 103}\n",
    "\n",
    "ids.add(104)                  # add a new item\n",
    "print(103 in ids)             # fast membership check -> True\n",
    "\n",
    "a = {1, 2, 3}\n",
    "b = {2, 3, 4}\n",
    "print(a & b)   # intersection -> items that appear in BOTH sets -> {2, 3}\n",
    "print(a | b)   # union -> all items from both sets combined -> {1, 2, 3, 4}"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "---\n",
    "## 3. List Comprehensions - Worked Examples"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Traditional loop way: build an empty list, then add to it one item at a time\n",
    "squares = []\n",
    "for x in range(10):          # x takes every value from 0 to 9\n",
    "    squares.append(x**2)     # add x squared to the list\n",
    "print(squares)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Same exact result, written as a list comprehension (one line)\n",
    "# Read it as: 'give me x**2, for every x in range(10)'\n",
    "squares = [x**2 for x in range(10)]\n",
    "print(squares)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# List comprehension WITH a filter condition (the 'if' at the end)\n",
    "# Read it as: 'give me x, for every x in range(20), but only if x is divisible by 2'\n",
    "evens = [x for x in range(20) if x % 2 == 0]\n",
    "print(evens)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "---\n",
    "## 4. Lambda Functions - Worked Examples"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# A lambda is a small, unnamed function written in one line: lambda input: expression\n",
    "square = lambda x: x**2\n",
    "print(square(5))   # calling it just like a normal function -> 25"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# map() applies a function to EVERY item in a list and returns the results\n",
    "numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4]\n",
    "doubled = list(map(lambda x: x * 2, numbers))  # apply 'double it' to each number\n",
    "print(doubled)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# filter() keeps only the items where the lambda returns True\n",
    "numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]\n",
    "evens = list(filter(lambda x: x % 2 == 0, numbers))  # keep only even numbers\n",
    "print(evens)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "---\n",
    "## Basic Python Words Used in Questions 1-10\n",
    "\n",
    "These are the important words and symbols used in the answers.\n",
    "\n",
    "- `=` means **store a value** in a variable. Example: `numbers = [1, 2, 3]`.\n",
    "- `[]` creates a **list**. A list stores many values together.\n",
    "- `for` means **repeat something** for each item.\n",
    "- `in` means **look inside** a group of items. Example: `for number in numbers`.\n",
    "- `range(1, 11)` creates numbers from 1 up to 10. The last number, 11, is not included.\n",
    "- `if` means **check a condition**. The code runs only when the condition is True.\n",
    "- `%` finds the **remainder** after division. If `number % 2 == 0`, the number is even.\n",
    "- `==` asks, **are these two values equal?** It is different from `=`.\n",
    "- `len()` counts the number of characters in a word or the number of items in a list.\n",
    "- `.lower()` changes letters to lowercase.\n",
    "- `.upper()` changes letters to uppercase.\n",
    "- `lambda` creates a small, one-line function.\n",
    "- `map()` applies a function to every item. We use `list()` to turn the result into a normal list.\n",
    "- `filter()` keeps only the items that pass a condition and removes the others.\n",
    "- `True` means yes or correct. `False` means no or not correct.\n",
    "- `** 2` means square a number. `** 3` means cube a number.\n",
    "- `print()` displays the result on the screen.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Example: reading a list comprehension\n",
    "\n",
    "`even_numbers = [number for number in range(1, 51) if number % 2 == 0]`\n",
    "\n",
    "Read it from left to right:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. Take a `number`.\n",
    "2. Take it `for` every number `in range(1, 51)`.\n",
    "3. Keep it `if` the remainder after dividing by 2 is 0.\n",
    "4. Put the kept numbers inside a list `[]`.\n",
    "\n",
    "## 5. Exercise - Part A: List Comprehensions (6 questions)\n",
    "\n",
    "Complete each cell below. See the PDF for a full explanation of what each one is asking."
   ]
  },
  {
